'''2.''' In ''Arthropoda'', as crustaceans, specifically, the externally projecting processes of the body or its limbs, which are supplied with venous blood (which is thus brought into contact with the air dissolved in water), and constitute a special respiratory organ. See cut under ''Podophthalmia.'' {{smaller|Other kinds of respiratory organs in arthropods are ''tracheo-branchiæ'', ''tracheæ'', and ''pulmonary sacs.'' See these words.}}

'''3.''' In ''Vermes'', any appendages of the head or body so modified as to act as a respiratory organ; the various processes which protrude or radiate from the head or other region of the body, and have, or are supposed to have, a respiratory function. See cut under ''Protula.''
{{smaller block|In . . . [''Amphinomidæ'', ''Eunicidæ'', and ''Terebellidæ''] the ''branchiæ'' are ciliated branched plumes or tufts attached to the dorsal surface of more or fewer of the somites. In [''Serpulidæ''] . . . they are exclusively attached to the anterior segment of the body, and present the form of two large plumes, each consisting of a principal stem, with many lateral branches.
{{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Invert., p. 210.}}}}

